The reaction shown is:

\[ \text{Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate} + \text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{3-phosphoglycerate} \]

This reaction is typically catalyzed by the enzyme glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase in glycolysis. This enzyme also plays a crucial role in the production of NADH, which is an important electron carrier used in cellular respiration to generate ATP.

If this step is removed or bypassed, the cell would lose the ability to produce NADH efficiently. Without NADH, the electron transport chain in cellular respiration would be impaired, leading to a decrease in ATP production. This would be detrimental to the cell because ATP is the primary energy currency of the cell.

Therefore, shortening the glycolytic pathway by removing this reaction would likely be harmful to the cell.

